h: that is **leakage**.\\n\", \"- Split by month: train January to August, validate September and October, keep November and December for the test.\\n\", \"- Slides: Class 3, \\\"Train-test contamination: validation rows in the training set make the score look perfect\\\".\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 2: Split by month (2 marks)\\n\", \"Make `train` (months 1 to 8) and `valid` (months 9 and 10) from `clean`, rows in the same order. Use `month`, never a random split.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Input:** `clean`. **Store:** `train`, `valid`.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"# Your code here. Store: train, valid\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"### Note: fit on the training rows only\\n\", \"- Every number learned from data (the imputation medians, the baseline mean) comes from `train` only, then is applied to `valid`.\\n\", \"- `make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\"), model)` does this: it learns in `fit` and reuses in `predict`.\\n\", \"- Learning from all rows lets the model see the rows it is judged on, so the error looks too small.\\n\", \"- Slides: Class 3, \\\"The pipeline rule: fit on the training set only, then apply the same numbers\\\".\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 3: Three models, MAE and RMSE (13 marks)\\n\", \"Fit each model on `train` only (columns `FEATURES`, target `TARGET`) and predict `valid`: `make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\"), model)` with `DummyRegressor()`, `LinearRegression()` and `DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=4, random_state=17)`.\\n\", \"Then the MAE and RMSE of each on `valid`.\\n\", \"Chart 2: a bar chart of the three MAE values, with a title, both axis names and the unit (kVarh).\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Input:** `train`, `valid`. **Store:** `baseline_pred`, `linear_pred`, `tree_pred`, `baseline_mae`, `linear_mae`, `tree_mae`, `baseline_rmse`, `linear_rmse`, `tree_rmse`.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"# Your code here. Store: baseline_pred, linear_pred, tree_pred, baseline_mae, linear_mae, tree_mae, baseline_rmse, linear_rmse, tree_rmse\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"### Note: logistic regression, confusion matrix, precision, recall\\n\", \"- **Logistic regression** gives each row a probability between 0 and 1 (`predict_proba(X)[:, 1]`); warn when it is at least 0.5.\\n\", \"- **Confusion matrix:** TP right warning, FP false alarm, FN missed warning, TN rightly none; `confusion_matrix(actual, warned, labels=[0, 1]).ravel()` gives tn, fp, fn, tp.\\n\", \"- **Precision** = TP / (TP + FP); **recall** = TP / (TP + FN); a zero denominator gives 0.\\n\", \"- Slides: Class 2, \\\"Logistic regression in three lines: score, probability, prediction\\\" and \\\"The confusion matrix counts true and false positives and negatives\\\".\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 4: Which readings should raise a warning? (3 marks)\\n\", \"A warning is a reading with `TARGET` at or above `LIMIT`. Fit `make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\"), StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000))` on `train` and store the probability of a warning for each `valid` row.\\n\", \"Warn when the probability is at least `THRESHOLD` (0.5), compare with the real warnings in `valid`, and store the counts, precision and recall (0 when a count is zero).\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Input:** `train`, `valid`. **Store:** `warning_proba`, `tp`, `fp`, `fn`, `tn`, `precision`, `recall`.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"# Your code here. Store: warning_proba, tp, fp, fn, tn, precision, recall\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 5: Choose one model\\n\", \"Use the supplied rule (MAE at most `MAX_MAE`, time at most `MAX_MS`, then the smallest MAE): `chosen_model = choose_model({\\\"baseline\\\": baseline_mae, \\\"linear\\\": linear_mae, \\\"tree\\\": tree_mae})`. Never use the test set to choose.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Input:** `baseline_mae`, `linear_mae`, `tree_mae`. **Store:** `chosen_model`.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"# Your code here. Store: chosen_model\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"### Note: open the test set once, then refit\\n\", \"- The validation set chooses the model; the test set gives **one honest estimate** of the chosen one.\\n\", \"- Looking at the test set and then changing your choice makes its score too good to trust.\\n\", \"- Refit on `train` and `valid` together (more data), then measure on `test_rows` once.\\n\", \"- Slides: Class 3, \\\"We choose max_depth on the validation set and keep the test set sealed\\\".\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 6: Open the test set once (2 marks)\\n\", \"After Task 5, run `test_rows = open_test_set(chosen_model)` ONCE. Refit the chosen model and the warning model on `train` and `valid` together, then store the MAE on `test_rows` and the recall of the warnings at 0.5.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Input:** `train`, `valid`, `chosen_model`. **Store:** `test_rows`, `test_mae`, `test_recall`.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"# Your code here. Store: test_rows, test_mae, test_recall\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 7: Part B, the decision memo form (15 automatic marks)\\n\", \"Fill in `MEMO` in the next cell, for the plant's energy manager. Use your own numbers, at least two decimals, in kVarh.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"| Field | Enter | Marks |\\n\", \"|---|---|---|\\n\", \"| `recommended_model` | `\\\"baseline\\\"`, `\\\"linear\\\"` or `\\\"tree\\\"`: the model the rule chose and you opened the test set with. | 3 |\\n\", \"| `validation_mae` | The validation MAE of each model, from your notebook. | 3 |\\n\", \"| `test_mae`, `test_recall` | The MAE and the warning recall (0 to 1) from your one test opening. | 2 |\\n\", \"| `improvement_over_baseline` | Baseline validation MAE minus your model's (0 if you recommend the baseline). | 1 |\\n\", \"| `limits` | Three different letters: the true limits of this study. One mark each. | 3 |\\n\", \"| `next_step` | One letter: the best next step. | 1 |\\n\", \"| `answer_choice`, `answer_test_set` | Two answers in your own words, 8 to 80 words each. One mark each. | 2 |\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Limits** (some statements are false):\\n\", \"- **A.** The test set is only November and December of the same year, so the test metrics are one estimate that may be luckier or unluckier than the truth.\\n\", \"- **B.** The model with the lowest training error is always the best model for new months.\\n\", \"- **C.** The client limits (the error limit and the time limit) and the warning limit are invented teaching numbers; a real client would set their own.\\n\", \"- **D.** The baseline model should be left out of the comparison because it uses no features.\\n\", \"- **E.** Because the test set was opened only once, the test metrics have no uncertainty at all.\\n\", \"- **F.** All the readings come from one steel plant in one year (2018), so the model may not hold for another plant or for later years.\\n\", \"- **G.** Fitting the imputer on the training set only makes the validation results unreliable.\\n\", \"- **H.** The validation set is only two months (September and October), so the validation errors could change a lot with two other months.\\n\", \"- **I.** The test MAE proves that the model will be this accurate in every month of every year.\\n\", \"- **J.** RMSE is always smaller than MAE, so MAE is the more severe measure and should be the only one reported.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Next step:**\\n\", \"- **A.** Collect readings from more years (or another plant) and check the chosen model again on months it has never seen before any decision.\\n\", \"- **B.** Move the test months into the training set so the model learns from more rows, and report the training error.\\n\", \"- **C.** Deploy the chosen model now, because the test MAE is below the client limit.\\n\", \"- **D.** Tune the maximum depth of the decision tree until its test MAE is lowest, then report that test MAE.\\n\", \"- **E.** Remove the baseline model from the report so the results look better.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Written answers:**\\n\", \"- `answer_choice`: In one or two sentences: why do you recommend this model? Use a number from the validation set and say what you compared it with.\\n\", \"- `answer_test_set`: In one or two sentences: why is the test set opened only once, at the very end, and never used to choose the model?\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"# Fill in every field; do not rename a field.\\n\", \"MEMO = {\\n\", \"    \\\"recommended_model\\\": \\\"\\\",                  # \\\"baseline\\\", \\\"linear\\\" or \\\"tree\\\"\\n\", \"    \\\"validation_mae\\\": {\\\"baseline\\\": None, \\\"linear\\\": None, \\\"tree\\\": None},   # your validation MAE values, in kVarh\\n\", \"    \\\"test_mae\\\": None,                         # the test MAE from your ONE opening, in kVarh\\n\", \"    \\\"test_recall\\\": None,                      # the test recall from your ONE opening (0 to 1)\\n\", \"    \\\"improvement_over_baseline\\\": None,        # baseline validation MAE minus your recommended model's, in kVarh\\n\", \"    \\\"limits\\\": [\\\"\\\", \\\"\\\", \\\"\\\"],                   # three different letters from the Limits list\\n\", \"    \\\"next_step\\\": \\\"\\\",                          # one letter from the Next step list\\n\", \"    \\\"answer_choice\\\": \\\"\\\",                      # one or two sentences\\n\", \"    \\\"answer_test_set\\\": \\\"\\\",                    # one or two sentences\\n\", \"}\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}], \"metadata\": {\"iuea_exam_version\": \"2026.11-ml-project-steel-v5-plain\", \"kernelspec\": {\"display_name\": \"Python 3\", \"language\": \"python\", \"name\": \"python3\"}}, \"nbformat\": 4, \"nbformat_minor\": 4}";
